<p>The renumbering is a genuine trap: a petition titled under &ldquo;Section 482&rdquo; now means pre-arrest bail, not quashing. Delhi High Court filings since July 2024 show both the Registry and litigants policing the distinction.</p>

<h2>What the BNSS text actually changed</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Section 438 CrPC (as amended)</th><th>Section 482 BNSS</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Statutory factor list</td><td>Enumerated: nature and gravity, antecedents, flight risk, humiliation-motivated accusation</td><td>Omitted — courts apply the same factors as settled law</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Interim protection mechanism</td><td>Express interim-order scheme with notice to the Public Prosecutor</td><td>Not reproduced; courts grant interim protection as incidental to the power</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Conditions</td><td>Interrogation availability, non-inducement, not leaving India, further conditions as under 437(3)</td><td>Substantially identical</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Exclusions</td><td>Gang rape of woman under sixteen etc. (as amended)</td><td>Corresponding exclusions for specified offences retained</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The omission of the factor list has had no practical liberalising effect: the considerations travelled from <em>Gurbaksh Singh Sibbia</em> through <em>Sushila Aggarwal</em> as constitutional common law, and courts cite them regardless of the statute&rsquo;s silence.</p>

<h2>The settled architecture that survives</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>No automatic time limit.</strong> Protection ordinarily endures to the end of trial; curtailment needs case-specific reasons.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>No blanket orders.</strong> Protection attaches to specified accusations, not to all future FIRs; the application must identify the apprehended case.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Filing of chargesheet does not extinguish protection.</strong> Anticipatory bail does not lapse merely because investigation concludes — though courts may require regular bail at an appropriate stage in serious offences.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Cancellation remains available.</strong> Breach of conditions, non-cooperation or new material can lead to cancellation on the State&rsquo;s or complainant&rsquo;s application.</div>
</div>

<h2>Practical notes for Delhi filings</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p><strong>Plead the &ldquo;reason to believe&rdquo;.</strong> A notice under Section 35(3) BNSS (the old 41A CrPC), a named FIR, or a complaint forwarded for investigation grounds the apprehension; vague fear does not.</p>
  <p><strong>Annex cooperation.</strong> Replies to police notices, appearance records and documents already supplied transform the application&rsquo;s complexion.</p>
  <p><strong>Prepare for conditions.</strong> Passport deposit, joining investigation on written notice, and no-contact clauses are the standard currency; resist only those that would render the protection illusory.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This explainer describes the statutory transition in general terms for information. It is not legal advice; the availability and terms of anticipatory bail depend on the offence and the record in each case.</div>
